WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:05.160
Now for the subqueries.

00:05.160 --> 00:07.560
There are many different types and categories.

00:07.560 --> 00:12.120
So now what we're going to do I'm going to show you an overview of all those types and categories.

00:12.120 --> 00:15.040
And then later we're going to deep dive into each of those types.

00:15.120 --> 00:20.040
So first of all, if you are thinking about the dependencies between the subquery and the main query,

00:20.040 --> 00:22.960
there is mainly two types of subqueries.

00:22.960 --> 00:25.640
We have the Non-correlated subquery.

00:25.680 --> 00:29.840
That means the subquery is independent from the main query.

00:29.840 --> 00:32.800
And the second type is the correlated subquery.

00:32.800 --> 00:34.080
It's exactly the opposite.

00:34.120 --> 00:37.400
The subquery cannot depend on the main query.

00:37.440 --> 00:39.520
Of course we can explain all those stuff in details.

00:39.520 --> 00:40.480
Don't worry about it.

00:40.480 --> 00:42.040
So this is the first group.

00:42.040 --> 00:48.200
Now there is another group on how to group up the subqueries depending on the result type.

00:48.200 --> 00:52.360
So I mean with this that the subquery has different output and results.

00:52.360 --> 00:54.840
For example we have scalar subquery.

00:54.880 --> 00:58.000
It returns only one single value or another type.

00:58.000 --> 01:00.360
It's called the row subquery.

01:00.400 --> 01:05.420
It can return multiple rows and the final type called the table subquery.

01:05.460 --> 01:09.780
It is a subquery that returns multiple rows and as well multiple columns.

01:09.820 --> 01:14.220
Now we come to the third way and the last way on how to categorize the subqueries.

01:14.220 --> 01:17.300
And this time based on the location and the clauses.

01:17.300 --> 01:22.740
So we are describing here where the subquery is going to be used within the main query.

01:22.740 --> 01:27.060
So we can use it in different locations and clauses like the Select clause.

01:27.100 --> 01:29.340
Or we can use it in the From clause.

01:29.340 --> 01:32.140
And this is the most common type for the subqueries.

01:32.140 --> 01:34.500
Or we can use it before joining tables.

01:34.500 --> 01:38.900
And we can use it in order to filter the data in the Where clause and in the Where clause.

01:38.900 --> 01:42.220
As we learned, there are two different sets of operators.

01:42.220 --> 01:48.260
We can use the subquery together with the comparison operators, the list greater equal and so on.

01:48.300 --> 01:54.620
Or we can use it with the logical operators like the in any all and exists.

01:54.620 --> 01:59.020
So now those are the different types and categories for the subqueries.

01:59.020 --> 02:01.500
And we're going to now deep dive into all of them.

02:01.500 --> 02:06.580
So now let's go and start with the easiest category the result types of the subqueries.
